  {
    "episode_id": 90234,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90234.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 26,
    "turn_text": "Well, you know, good and bad, but that's like that in any place, right? That's like that inside the GOP. With any place, you'll find people that are there that really want to do the right things. And you'll find people that are there just to make sure that they get reelected. And I tend to work with those that are really willing to do the right thing. As a matter of fact, Marshall Fudge, who was on workforce and education, is a co-sponsor of my affordable college act, which is to remove regulation that brings the cost of higher education down. We are finding that as tuition rises far faster than the rate of inflation, that it's making it difficult for middle-income families to poor families and everything in between. It's very difficult for them for their children to be able to get an education. with the rising cost of tuition. So this is something where she realized that we wanted to do something together that would drive down the cost of higher education and she jumped on with me. You know, there are people there that we're working with. One of the goals that I said I was going to do everything I can to bring people to come along with me. And we're making some inroads. For a long time, there have been a lot of people, there's been no one to push up against those ideas that they all have together. And so when you push up against some of those ideas and you say, how about this? Will this work a little bit better? You know, some people will listen and say, hi, I never thought about it like this before. And I think, again, it's not always the most comfortable place to be in, but you have to put yourself in uncomfortable situations in order to really lead.",
